103
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Now, sometimes people ask to have their names withheld because we deal with sensitive subjects sometimes here. and that's true of this first email question,

104
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:26,000
And it reads like this: can a troop decide to do Eagle Courts of Honor for several Eagle candidates at once and not permit parties at the same time?

105
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:38,000
The troop combines several scouts into one Eagle Court of Honor. They buy a couple of cakes, but they ask families to go elsewhere if they want to have a big celebration or a party.

106
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:49,000
The troop meets at a church. Three of the scouts getting their Eagle for this Court of Honor attend another denomination and they would like to have their Court of Honor at that church.

107
00:10:49,000 --> 00:11:00,000
So, once again, can a troop committee dictate this sort of thing? Can they say when and where a court of honor is to be held? Any help is greatly appreciated.

108
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,000
By the way, love your site and the emails.

109
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Well, that's a fairly easy answer. Yes, the troop committee can choose how to define how Courts of Honor are held, where they're held and whether or not there's going to be a big celebration and a party afterward.

110
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Now the piece that you may be missing about this whole thing is what happens if the troop committee doesn't define those things.

111
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:32,000
I know from experience that without some definition, things can get out of hand pretty fast and there can be a lot of misunderstandings.

112
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:40,000
So a troop committee really should kind of think their way through exactly what they want to have happen around Courts of Honor.

113
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:54,000
If you put yourself in the troop committee's shoes for a moment, you may see that what they're trying to do with these definitions is attempt to treat every scout fairly, to prevent overburdening the troop schedule.

114
00:11:54,000 --> 00:12:03,000
If you have more than three Eagle Scouts at a given time and you do separate Courts of Honor for each and every one, that could really present a problem.

115
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:11,000
It's a wonderful problem to have. So I can see the wisdom in what they're doing by asking all the Scouts to combine in one court of honor,

116
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
And they're also trying to minimize what I have seen in my own troop before

117
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:24,000
And that is families kind of playing the one upsmanship and trying to trump one another about who can throw the biggest party.

118
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:30,000
That's really happened, And boy, that can cause some difficult situations too.

119
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Now, that being said, that a troop committee can make a decision about these things and lend some definition to it, it would be perfectly appropriate and probably smooth things over with everyone involved.

120
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:49,000
If you have a presentation at the other church in addition to the other Court of Honor, okay,

121
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:54,000
There's nothing that says a scout can't have a couple of different presentations of his Eagle,

122
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
And I have done just that in the past.

123
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:03,000
One of my Scouts wanted his Eagle presented during a service at his church and they were happy to have that happen

124
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
And I was happy to attend and I did that for him.

125
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,000
The understanding that we had with him and his family was that he could invite whoever he liked,

126
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:19,000
But we wouldn't compel anybody to attend that separate service because he was having a troop Court of Honor as well

127
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
And he was recognized at that troop Court of Honor with two other Eagle candidates

128
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:30,000
And he also had the other presentation during a church service and I was happy to go do that.

129
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:35,000
You can avoid a lot of possible conflicts and bad feelings about these things

130
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
By having the troop committee sit down and kind of think their way through exactly how this should work

131
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:57,000
And then also make the offer that somebody from the troop will go- and hopefully it would be the scout master- would go and preside over a second presentation at a location at a time the Scouts family requests.

132
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:02,000
So once I sent that advice out, I got this reply. thanks so much.

133
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,000
I appreciate your input. I'll pass it on to the parents who voice concerns. much appreciation for what you do.

134
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Well, I really hope it helps. I'd like to hear how it all works out.

135
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Here's another email that came in. if a senior patrol leader is not performing up to par and the Scouts are unhappy with that performance,

136
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
How does he get removed?

137
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
Well, it's a fairly simple question, isn't it?

138
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
How did he become a senior patrol leader in the first place?

139
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:34,000
The troop had an election and they elected him as senior patrol leader, right?

140
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:43,000
If the Scouts are unhappy and they think that it's time for another election to elect a new senior patrol leader, they can do that,

141
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
And the same is true for patrol leaders.

142
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:51,000
So you know, I think it's a fairly easy situation to deal with.

143
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,000
It might be a little uncomfortable and there may be some disappointment involved,

144
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:02,000
But if you're a wise Scouter, you're going to counsel everybody through this and you're going to put as positive a face on it as possible.

145
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:06,000
The reply I got back from that particular email was: thank you.

146
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:14,000
This is exactly what I've told the other adults and Scouts who've expressed the concerns to me about our senior patrol leader.

147
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,000
So far, no one has decided to do anything about it.

148
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:23,000
I think what they want me to do as Scoutmaster is remove him, and I'm not going to do it.

149
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:27,000
I continue to support and nurture him, even though he doesn't apply himself right now.

150
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
He may turn the corner yet

151
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
And you know what I really support you in that

152
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:36,000
I have had 30 years worth of senior patrol leaders.

153
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Some of them take a long time to catch on and it takes a lot of nurturing and working with them.

154
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:50,000
And yes, I've had adults and parents and Scouts kind of give me the stink eye about. well, you know you really should do something about this

155
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
And my reply is always: look, he was elected.

156
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
If you want to have another election, you can do it.

157
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:02,000
What they want is they usually want the Scoutmaster to be the bad guy and to make the change.

158
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
But the Scoutmaster didn't appoint the senior patrol leader. He was elected.

159
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
You want to have another election? Let's go.

160
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:17,000
If you don't want to do that, well, we're going to continue to work with this guy and we're going to try and help him find what he needs to find to be a really good, effective senior patrol leader.

161
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
I've had that happen in the past.

162
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:25,000
It can be a little frustrating, but if you keep working at it, most of them will step up and make things happen.

163
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:31,000
Heard from Allison Benton, who said: hi, I'm an assistant Scoutmaster. We recently got our second patrol.

164
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Now I'm excited about this because it means we're growing,

165
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:38,000
We have two patrols and we can now elect a senior patrol leader.

166
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
But how do you do that? Is there a correct way of electing a senior patrol leader?

167
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
I need a little advice here.

168
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Well, Allison, there's really no right or wrong way to have the election, so don't worry about getting it wrong.

169
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:56,000
What I would suggest to you is go to the Scouts, get them together, tell them they have two patrols now

170
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
and that this means that they can elect a senior patrol leader if they want to,

171
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
And they probably will want to

172
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Ask them how they want to do it and what they think makes a good choice for a senior patrol leader.

173
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
That question should start a pretty good discussion.

174
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Ask them what they'll do to make sure the election is fair and above board and how the candidates will be chosen

175
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:19,000
and exactly how voting happens and everything.

176
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And if you guide them through that process of discovering these things for themselves,

177
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:26,000
they're going to be pretty sure that it was their idea in the first place.

178
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:29,000
They're going to be very excited and empowered by it.

179
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Tell them to have the election and let you know how they chose and then walk away and let them at it.

180
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
That's what I would suggest doing.

181
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Once you've had this discussion with the Scouts, you're going to create a tradition in the troop

182
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
of how the senior patrol leader is elected

183
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
And, like I said, get them to explain exactly how it's going to happen and everything like that.

184
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
You may have to give them a little direction if they don't seem to get it exactly right,

185
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,000
But then walk away, let them have their election and tell you who the new senior patrol leader is.

