00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I feel like I'm doing it all wrong and I don't know what to do different.

199
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Either I'm not doing my best or my best is inadequate,

200
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
But I just can't walk away as many times as that crosses my mind.

201
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
I'm sorry, I'm kind of venting here.

202
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
I do appreciate your podcast and your blog

203
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:57,000
And it gives me enough inspiration to plot on ahead for another week.

204
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Well, everybody's been there,

205
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Every single Scouter. if you stay around long enough as a Scouter,

206
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:08,000
you're going to have those dark nights of the soul where you wonder if anything is going right.

207
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:14,000
You're wondering why nobody will help and you're getting a very strong urge just to chuck it all in.

208
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:19,000
So what? you're looking for some advice on how to be an effective volunteer

209
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and how to get other people to help you make things happen.

210
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:28,000
So let's start thinking about how you can turn into the orchestra leader.

211
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:32,000
rather than running around trying to play every single instrument on your own,

212
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:35,000
You've got to get some people in the seats helping you right.

213
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
So step one is understanding the problem.

214
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
What you told me, it's obvious.

215
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:44,000
Like many good souls, you feel that you must be personally responsible for everything.

216
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Now, that is partially true, and that partial truth is what leads many of us-

217
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
And I'm saying us, because I've been there before,

218
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:59,000
as have just about every Scouter that's listening at this very moment has been there before too.

219
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
We tend to take on too much.

220
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:05,000
There's a big difference between being responsible for everything

221
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and making everything happen.

222
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:12,000
So step one is differentiating between those two things.

223
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
I'm sure you understand what I'm saying.

224
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:17,000
The trouble is that there's usually this barrier of fear

225
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:22,000
or trepidation that keeps most of us from soliciting and accepting help.

226
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,000
We're pretty sure in the back of our minds that people won't do

227
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:30,000
what we're asking them to do as well as we can do it,

228
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,000
or they will simply not do what we're asking at all.

229
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:38,000
So let's understand that we all feel that way, at least to some extent,

230
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
and we need to get over that.

231
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:44,000
So step number two is writing down all that you do,

232
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,000
Make it like a task list, a to-do list, and then triage that list.

233
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:54,000
What must you continue doing personally and what can you delegate?

234
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:59,000
You need to get that list of what you must do, down to two or three items

235
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and everything else you're going to delegate out.

236
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,000
Step number three is overcoming the fear of disappointment

237
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and handing things off to others.

238
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:12,000
You have to take these things off your list and not pick them up again.

239
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:15,000
If someone doesn't get the job done properly to your lights,

240
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:21,000
or they just drop it, step back, take a few deep breaths and move on.

241
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Find somebody else,

242
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,000
Or you may find that that thing that you thought was terribly important,

243
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
that had to be done, really didn't interest anybody

244
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
and didn't need to be done in the first place.

245
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:34,000
Now, if you don't do this, you're not going to last long.

246
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:38,000
You'll collapse and the whole program will collapse around your exit,

247
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:39,000
and that happens.

248
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:44,000
So it becomes your responsibility to make sure that you're looking

249
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:45,000
very hard at what you're doing,

250
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
You're triaging that list,

251
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:51,000
You're delegating it out and you're not picking it up again.

252
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:56,000
This all sounds very simple, but I know how difficult it can be.

253
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:59,000
I also want to address one thing.

254
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:03,000
Usually scouts are hyper-responsible people.

255
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:07,000
They see the need, they step in and they feel the need,

256
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and then they are amazed and dismayed at the fact

257
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:14,000
that nobody else seems to understand that there's a need there

258
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and wants to help out.

259
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:20,000
Now the natural reaction to that is to be a little discouraged

260
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
with human nature.

261
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
All these parents, their children, are participating

262
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
in this wonderful program.

263
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:29,000
You're doing the yeoman's work of keeping everything going

264
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,000
and they just seem to sit there, pay no attention

265
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:35,000
to what you're doing, not really appreciate it too much

266
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:38,000
and have no idea that it takes a lot of effort.

267
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:42,000
And so when we feel that way, naturally you get discouraged.

268
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
Naturally you want to pitch it all in

269
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
because nobody really cares.

270
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
Well, I've got some news for you.

271
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,000
People really do care, but people really do not understand,

272
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:55,000
on the same level that you do, the kind of effort

273
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,000
and involvement that is needed to make things like this happen.

274
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:02,000
We think, because of our own hyper-responsibility

275
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
and the way that we see things, that everybody else

276
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
is like us and sees the same thing,

277
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
and even though they realize we need help,

278
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
they're just going to stand by and say,

279
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
well, yeah, I don't really care.

280
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:15,000
That's not true. They don't realize that you need help.

281
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:19,000
So you have to express that very specifically

282
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and, like I said, write down that list of tasks.

283
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:26,000
you triage the list, you find out what you need help with.

284
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:29,000
You don't make a general appeal to a crowd of people.

285
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:32,000
You go to an individual and you say:

286
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
this is what I need you to do,

287
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
Can you help me with this?

288
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:37,000
And you know what.

289
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
Most of the time you're going to get a good reaction.

290
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
Well, I didn't know you needed help with that.

291
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
I'd be happy to give you a hand.

292
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:46,000
Now that might be an oversimplification of the whole process,

293
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:49,000
but that's basically the big, broad, underlying concepts

294
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
about the way that it works.

295
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
I heard back from the person who sent me that email

296
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:57,000
and they said: you know, I actually had a decent PAC meeting.

297
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:58,000
I did what you said.

298
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:01,000
I wrote out a job description of our Scouting for Food Coordinator.

299
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:04,000
I asked a specific person and they said, yes,

300
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:08,000
it was all of the theory in action. a specific request,

301
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:13,000
well described. ask a single, specific person instead of casting a net.

302
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:16,000
give them enough lead time to put everything together

303
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
and then step back and let them at it.

304
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
My weakness is procrastination.

305
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:21,000
I put off asking.

306
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
I don't have time to write the description.

307
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:26,000
I feel guilty for approaching people with a vague request

308
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:27,000
on a short deadline,

309
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:30,000
So I just end up doing it myself and I get stressed out.

310
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:33,000
Here's hoping I can get ahead on the documentation

311
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:36,000
and approaching folks about specific things that need to be done.

312
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,000
Thanks again for your help and for the resources

313
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:40,000
that you've created.

314
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:44,000
Well, I think that that answer describes exactly

315
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:45,000
what I was talking about.

316
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
You have to be specific.

317
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:48,000
You have to go to individuals,

318
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
You have to ask and you know

319
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
you need to give them enough lead time to do it.

320
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
but sometimes the lead time won't be there

321
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:55,000
and you explain that

322
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:58,000
You say: look, I'm kind of up to my elbows here

323
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
and I need some help.

324
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
I know that this is a short notice,

325
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
but can you help me with this?

326
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
You're going to find a lot of people are there

327
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:07,000
willing to help.

328
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
They're just waiting for you to ask.

329
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
I got this email from a patrol leader who said:

330
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:15,000
can backyard camping fulfill one of the overnight campouts

331
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:16,000
to reach first class rank?

332
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
I'm going to be planning a winter camp out

333
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:21,000
at our scout camp and another one in June or July,

334
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:22,000
but we need to have a third one

335
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
to get everybody to first class.

336
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:25,000
Thanks for your help.

337
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
Well, as the patrol leader,

338
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
you ought to be able to plan and carry out a camping trip

339
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:32,000
that qualifies for one of the three overnight activities

340
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:33,000
for first class.

341
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
There's nothing that says this camp

342
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:38,000
being somebody's backyard. overnight activities

343
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:41,000
with patrols do require one scouter

344
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
who has youth protection training

345
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
and at least one other adult to be present,

346
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
but you can certainly make it happen

347
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
And, like I said, camping is camping.

348
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
If it's in the backyard, it's in the backyard.

349
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
I don't see anything that says it has to be camping

350
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:57,000
in a certain place or a certain type of place.

351
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
I'm always happy to hear from patrol leaders

352
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
or senior patrol leaders or other youth members.

353
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:05,000
I want to note, though, that because you're a youth member,

