note what it says:

455
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
When he believes he's completed

456
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
all the requirements for a rank,

457
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
including a Scoutmaster conference,

458
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:01,000
a Board of Review must be granted.

459
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
It's not any question about that.

460
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
It must be granted.

461
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,000
Scoutmasters or councils or districts-

462
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:11,000
in the case of the Eagle Scout rank, for example-

463
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:14,000
do not have the authority to expect a boy

464
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:17,000
to request or organize a Board of Review,

465
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:20,000
or defer to him or ask him to perform

466
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:22,000
beyond the requirements

467
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
in order to be granted a Board of Review.

468
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:25,000
So there you go.

469
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
This isn't really subject to opinion

470
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
or how anybody feels.

471
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:31,000
It's right there in black and white

472
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,000
And I don't see any other way of interpreting it

473
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
other than what's happening in your troop right now

474
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:38,000
is not in accordance with advancement policy.

475
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:41,000
So once everybody's read and understood this,

476
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:43,000
you can toss away your Board of Review forms

477
00:30:43,000 --> 00:30:44,000
and all that stuff

478
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
and stop adding to the requirements.

479
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
And now, requiring a scout

480
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
to kind of jump through that request

481
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:53,000
of Board of Review hoop is a fairly common practice,

482
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:56,000
but, as we can see in the Guide to Advancement,

483
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
it is altogether forbidden.

484
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
In the process of looking at this,

485
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:02,000
you might want to look at everything else

486
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:03,000
that's happening

487
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
and you might find a few other things

488
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:06,000
that are out of sync.

489
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:07,000
Everybody ought to sit down,

490
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:08,000
read that Guide to Advancement,

491
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:10,000
make sure that they're pointed to true north

492
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:13,000
and that they're adhering to those policies.

493
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:15,000
You'll see that the advancement program

494
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:16,000
is all about helping scouts,

495
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:18,000
not throwing barriers in their way.

496
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,000
but I suspect, James,

497
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:22,000
that you already know that

498
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:23,000
Heard from Ranjit Sudan,

499
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
who is an assistant scout master

500
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:28,000
with Troop 4002 in Oak Park, Illinois.

501
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,000
Ranjit says

502
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:32,000
we have a fairly unique situation.

503
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
We've recently had a recruiting year

504
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:38,000
and I have 16 new young 11-year-old scouts

505
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:39,000
joining a troop

506
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:41,000
which, prior to their arrival,

507
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:44,000
numbered only 22 to 24 active scouts.

508
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:47,000
Well, Ranjit, I would agree it's fairly unique,

509
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
but it's actually more common than you think.

510
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:51,000
You're about doubling your troop,

511
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:53,000
which is a great problem to have, isn't it?

512
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:55,000
We kind of find ourselves

513
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:57,000
in this wag the dog situation.

514
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,000
The troop has been boy-led,

515
00:31:59,000 --> 00:32:01,000
but this bounty has many of us-

516
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:04,000
including, maybe, and especially the older boys- on edge.

517
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
To compound matters,

518
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,000
two of the newcomers have special needs.

519
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:10,000
I'd appreciate your advice on balancing

520
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:12,000
the boy's natural desire

521
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:14,000
to be in a patrol with their buddies

522
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:17,000
with making sure that they all find a niche.

523
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:20,000
The need and desire for social engineering

524
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:24,000
is hard for many of us adults to resist.

525
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:26,000
So, Ranjit, I would say this:

526
00:32:26,000 --> 00:32:28,000
First of all, let's understand

527
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:32,000
that being youth-led, being scout-led,

528
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:35,000
isn't really a destination we travel towards

529
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:37,000
with any hope of arriving

530
00:32:37,000 --> 00:32:40,000
or a building project that is ever going to be finished.

531
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:42,000
It's a continuous journey and an ongoing project

532
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,000
that really is never completed.

533
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:46,000
Now you describe a challenging situation,

534
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:49,000
but if things are going as they should.

535
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:52,000
you will always have some kind of challenging situation.

536
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:54,000
This is a pretty big one, I admit,

537
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,000
but there's always going to be

538
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:58,000
this little edge of uncertainty

539
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:00,000
and kind of chaos involved.

540
00:33:00,000 --> 00:33:02,000
We never arrive at a place

541
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:04,000
of total stability and efficiency,

542
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:06,000
because that's not what we're aimed at creating.

543
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:08,000
We're aimed at creating opportunities

544
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:10,000
for young people to learn how to be responsible,

545
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:12,000
to learn the Scout Oath and Law,

546
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:14,000
to become good human beings.

547
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:17,000
That's not ever going to be a stable situation, is it?

548
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:19,000
It's always going to be changing

549
00:33:19,000 --> 00:33:21,000
because young people are involved in it.

550
00:33:21,000 --> 00:33:23,000
Now, having this big crowd of new scouts

551
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
is challenging and it's going to be difficult

552
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
for everybody involved.

553
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
It kind of upsets what you thought

554
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:31,000
was a pretty stable situation,

555
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:33,000
But it's also a fantastic opportunity

556
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
for developing youth leadership

557
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,000
and youth responsibility.

558
00:33:37,000 --> 00:33:39,000
So what I would suggest you do

559
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
is sit down with your youth leaders,

560
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
ask them lots of questions

561
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:45,000
that are posed to get them thinking

562
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:47,000
about this situation and forming strategies

563
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
to meet the challenge.

564
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:51,000
How do you guys think the new Scouts are doing?

565
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,000
Do you see any problems or difficulties

566
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:55,000
that our patrols are chosen?

567
00:33:55,000 --> 00:33:57,000
Do you think we should make any changes to that?

568
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
Open that discussion with your older Scouts

569
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
and see what they have to say.

570
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:03,000
They're going to have some fantastic ideas.

571
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,000
Now, if they're just used to adults

572
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
intervening and fixing things,

573
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,000
they might just stare at you

574
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:11,000
and wonder what you're talking about.

575
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:13,000
In fact, they may rather resent the implication

576
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:15,000
that this is something that they have to do,

577
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:17,000
that they would rather have the adults

578
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:19,000
take care of it. right,

579
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,000
But you have to explain

580
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:23,000
the responsibility to make decisions,

581
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
and the adults involved are going to help,

582
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
support and back them up,

583
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:29,000
But they're not going to make those decisions for them.

584
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,000
And once they get this,

585
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:33,000
they're going to start suggesting changes.

586
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
Some are going to be promising ideas,

587
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:37,000
some maybe not so promising,

588
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:39,000
but that's where you continue asking questions,

589
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,000
getting them to think their way

590
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
through things.

591
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,000
That process of mentoring and guidance

592
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:47,000
will ultimately result in resolutions

593
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:49,000
to the situation you describe.

594
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
Now, once they have a plan of action,

595
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
walk away from things and let them

596
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:55,000
start putting that plan into place.

597
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
After they've started making those changes,

598
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
it's time to follow up

599
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,000
with another discussion and another round of questions.

600
00:35:01,000 --> 00:35:03,000
So how are things working?

601
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,000
Are they working as you expected?

602
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:07,000
What's working and what isn't?

603
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:09,000
What are you going to do to further change the situation

604
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:11,000
Once you get in the habit of asking questions

605
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:13,000
like this, rather than making statements

606
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,000
or explaining things, your youth leaders

607
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:17,000
will begin finding the answers

608
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:19,000
and hearing things out for themselves,

609
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:21,000
And that's a pretty exciting thing,

610
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:23,000
Question after question,

611
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:25,000
asked in a spirit of honest curiosity,

612
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:27,000
a lot of listening to answers

613
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:29,000
and unlocking resolutions and potential.

614
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:31,000
I understand that this is a big challenge,

615
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:33,000
but it's one that's very exciting

616
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:35,000
and has tremendous possibilities

617
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:37,000
attached to it.

618
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:39,000
So do let me know how things go.

619
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:41,000
That's a lot of email questions for one podcast.

620
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,000
If you have a question and you want to get in touch with me,

621
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,000
you're going to find out how to do that

622
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:47,000
in just a moment.

623
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,000
That's right. it's time

624
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:13,000
for a Scoutmasters Minute

625
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:17,000
My hand.

626
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:25,000
I want to make sure you go to

627
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:27,000
scoutmasterscgcom and you read the post

628
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:29,000
called

629
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:31,000
Tropic Castigades

630
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:33,000
and the Scout Law,

631
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:35,000
Because in that post there's a video

632
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:37,000
that I really want you to see

633
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:39,000
that explains this incredible phenomenon.

634
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:41,000
In 1995,

635
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:43,000
wolves were reintroduced

636
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:45,000
into Yellowstone National Park.

637
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:47,000
Now, to make this very simple,

638
00:36:47,000 --> 00:36:49,000
this caused what is called

639
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:51,000
a tropic cascade,

640
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:53,000
Because wolves prey on elk,

641
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:55,000
Elk feed on aspen trees.

642
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,000
When there are fewer elk,

643
00:36:57,000 --> 00:36:59,000
there get to be more aspen trees

644
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:01,000
and the aspen trees grow bigger.

645
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:03,000
More aspen trees and bigger aspen trees

646
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:05,000
mean that now there are more beavers

647
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:07,000
who feed on the aspen

648
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:09,000
and build with them.

649
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:11,000
More beavers mean more beaver dams,

650
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:13,000
and beaver dams change the course of rivers

651
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:15,000
and they change the environment.

652
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,000
More aspen trees also stabilize

653
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:19,000
the river banks

654
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:21,000
and this changes the course of rivers.

655
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,000
So it's amazing, but it's true,

656
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:25,000
that reintroducing wolves

657
00:37:25,000 --> 00:37:27,000
to Yellowstone nearly 20 years ago

658
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:29,000
has changed the course

659
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,000
of the rivers in Yellowstone.

660
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:33,000
Isn't that something

661
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:35,000
Scientists call this natural system?

662
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:37,000
I've just described a

663
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:39,000
tropic cascade,

664
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,000
and tropic refers to the food chain,

665
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:43,000
and cascade describes

666
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:45,000
how top level predators like wolves

667
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:47,000
affect other species

668
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,000
like elk down the food chain

669
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:51,000
and how that relationship

670
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:53,000
affects the very environment

671
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:55,000
that they all share.

672
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:57,000
Now every scout has the chance to go camping

673
00:37:57,000 --> 00:37:59,000
and see the great cycles of nature,

674
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:01,000
like tropic cascades, up close.

675
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:03,000
I would say hopefully,

676
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:05,000
maybe not seeing top level predators

677
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:07,000
up close, okay,

678
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:09,000
But we learn a lot about ourselves

679
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:11,000
from observing

680
00:38:11,000 --> 00:38:13,000
the natural world.

681
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:15,000
The tropic cascade that wolves caused

682
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:17,000
in Yellowstone makes me pause to think

683
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:19,000
for a moment about

684
00:38:19,000 --> 00:38:21,000
the same kind of cascading effect

685
00:38:21,000 --> 00:38:23,000
of what we say

686
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:25,000
and what we do.

687
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:27,000
It's all there in the Scout Oath and Law, isn't it?

688
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:29,000
When we're trustworthy, loyal, helpful,

689
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:31,000
friendly, courteous, kind, obedient,

690
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:33,000
cheerful, thrifty, brave,

691
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:35,000
clean and reverent.

692
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:37,000
our own happiness

693
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:39,000
cascades down to those around us.

694
00:38:39,000 --> 00:38:41,000
Our actions and decisions

695
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:43,000
affect our environment.

696
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:45,000
They create opportunity.

697
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:47,000
They influence others.

698
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:49,000
They affect all of our relationships.

699
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:51,000
Wolves occupy a specific role

700
00:38:51,000 --> 00:38:53,000
in nature. They don't make many rational

701
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:55,000
choices, do they?

702
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,000
But we get to make rational choices

703
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:59,000
as individuals every day.

704
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:01,000
What we choose to say

705
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:03,000
and how we choose to act

706
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:05,000
can have either negative

707
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:07,000
or positive cascading effects.

708
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:09,000
So when

709
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:11,000
Scouts make a choice to follow

710
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:13,000
the Scout Oath and Law,

711
00:39:13,000 --> 00:39:15,000
the cascade that follows

712
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:17,000
always has a positive effect on their lives

713
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:19,000
and everything around them.

714
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:35,000
Well, thanks so much

715
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:37,000
for listening to this

716
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:39,000
239th edition

717
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,000
of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

